A||||2||Michael||Timming problems?||||1||
Z||000000||Michael||09-06-2000||08:54 PM||||I just got a 85GT for $500 and drove it for the first time tonight, everything is good but how do you check and adjust the timming w/ a timming light?  I know that you have to take an average of two cylinders but how dow you see the timming marks onthe flywheel( not much space down there)!!!!This is my first Fiero so any help would be great.  The car needs some TLC but it has alot of potiential!  Hope to have a show quality car by the time i`m finished.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Heisenhund||09-06-2000||09:18 PM||||Welcome aboard. You'll like this place!<P>As far as timing goes, I found it was easier to take the right side vent off and clean off the cover that the marks are on. When you hit it with the light, you'll see the timing mark on the crank pulley. You average cylinders 1 & 4.<P>BTW it is a bit of a hassle to reach all the way across the engine to adjust the distributor. And I had a problem with one of the distributor connections hitting the EGR tube blanket. Had to trim it a bit.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000002||StuGood||09-07-2000||12:54 PM||||The timing mark for setting ignition timing is on the harmonic damper (crank pulley end of things; passenger side), not the flywheel.  I bet you knew this, but thought I'd mention it since you said flywheel in your post  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<P>FWIW, first time I get good access to the damper (such as when replacing/tightening belts), I clean the damper, sand lightly, and apply a little patch of screaming orange enamel on either side of the timing slot.  That way, next time I adjust the timing, it's a breeze.  Warning: there's more than one slot on the damper  - mark the widest one.<P>Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">         ||||reg||4
